[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: A colour means one thing across the whole page; each system keeps its own words for it

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-COLOUR-IS-SHARED-WORDS-ARE-NOT
TITLE: A colour means one thing across the whole page; each system keeps its own words for it
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATUS: accepted
RETIRES_ON: A system needs a verdict no declared colour can carry, or two systems need the same colour for genuinely opposed meanings.
STATEMENT: >>>
COLOUR is shared across the whole page and WORDS belong to each system.

[LINK: NAR-CANON-COLOURS] is a durable table in the spec: one row per colour
the page may draw, and the one thing that colour means wherever it appears. A
system's legend keeps naming its own verdicts -- a design review's words are
not a renderer's -- but every word it declares must sit on a colour whose
declared meaning fits it, and no colour may mean two things.

Where a screen shows more than one system, the page draws the shared colour key
beside them: each colour, its meaning, and the word each enabled system uses
for it. That is what lets a reader carry a colour from one system's rows to
another's without learning three vocabularies.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Three legends existed, one per root, and nothing said what a colour meant
outside the document that declared it. Green already meant "stands as written",
"retyped to a requirement", "in force" and "code landed"; teal meant "records
what was observed", "designed, not built" and "the layer this page is about".
While every document owned its own tab that was survivable, because a reader
only ever saw one legend at a time. Under
[LINK: DEC-TABS-ARE-A-DECLARED-VOCABULARY] a tab is the union of every enabled
system, so two or three vocabularies land on one screen and colour stops
carrying information.

The operator was given three ways out -- shared colours with per-system words,
one page-wide vocabulary, or leaving the legends alone -- and chose the first.
One page-wide vocabulary was rejected as flattening real distinctions: the
verdicts of a design review, the solidity of a semantic layer and the build
state of a renderer are not one scale.

What the colours already were is most of the argument that this costs little.
Reading the three legends against each other, sixteen of the seventeen words
were ALREADY on a colour consistent with this table; only HERE, a locator
rather than a verdict, had to move, from teal to grey. The collision was in the
prose, not in the assignments -- so what was missing was a place to write the
shared meaning down, which is what this decision adds.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Ruled by the operator on 2026-08-30, choosing between three options put to them as a card. AUTHORED_BY reads llm because the writer has no flag for the human rungs.
<<<
